  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 151 PM EDT Sat Jul 25 2026\n\n- Several Chances For Thunderstorms Tonight Through Monday Night \n  With Severe Weather Possible\n\nBottom line up front. Several chances for strong to severe \nthunderstorms exist Sunday into Monday Night. However, confidence in \ntiming and coverage of any storms remains low.\n\nFirst off. a shortwave crosses the area tonight along with placement \nof LFQ jet forcing. This will bring potential for showers and maybe \nisolated non-severe storms. Given the dry ambient airmass, coverage \nwill be poor with most areas having a 20% or less chance of rain \ntonight.\n\nAttention then turns to Sunday afternoon as MLCIN erodes with the \narrival of a shortwave and meso-low development across interior MI. \nBest erosion of MLCIN will be near and east of US-127, however \nwithout much in the way of mesoscale forcing, coverage is \nquestionable. In addition, forecast profiles in some short range \nmodel guidance show a mid-level cap which would inhibit storm \ndevelopment. However, if storms can develop, shear and instability \nis sufficient for damaging winds and hail.\n\nSome short range guidance shows the potential for one or more storm \nclusters to develop in warm air advection Sunday Night/early Monday. \nIf/where this happens is low confidence which is common in \"Ring of \nFire\" scenarios like this. Any storms will be capable of damaging \nwinds and large hail.\n\nThe greatest concern for an organized severe weather threat is \nMonday afternoon/evening as a cold front moves through the area. \nWhat occurs as this happens is dependent on if/to what extent we see \nany morning storms which will affect our ability to destabilize. \nHowever, both a strong mid-level jet (with attendant forcing) and an \nincreasing low-level jet in the evening will provide ample low-level \nand deep layer shear that given sufficient instability, severe \nweather is possible with any clusters or MCSs that develop. The \nfavored area for this at present is the southern 2/3 of our forecast \narea. Damaging winds would be the main threat with all hazards \npossible.\n\nThe caveat as alluded to earlier is this is a highly uncertain \nsetup. Where exactly these clusters develop and track is tough to \npredict more than 12-24 hours out. There are real chances like has \nhappened other days over the last few weeks that storms fail to \ndevelop, or that they miss our area keeping us dry. Each subsequent \nround of storms also depends on what happens with previous storms \nand any potential boundaries or debris clouds. However, the \nenvironment is favorable for storms to be severe if they develop.\n\n- Dry and Cooler Mid to Late Week\n\nConditions dry out going into Tuesday as the cold front clears the \narea. Upper-level troughing and surface ridging keeps us a bit \ncooler as well as dry into Thursday. Precipitation chances then \nreturn Friday as a shortwave swings through the area.",
